<div class="wp-block-column is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow" style="flex-basis:64%">
<h1 class="sp-title wp-block-post-title">Apple MacBook Pro Comparison: M4 Pro vs M5 Pro</h1>

<div class="wp-block-group sp-byline is-layout-flex wp-block-group-is-layout-flex">
<div class="wp-block-post-author-name">Adam Talks Tech</div>
<div class="wp-block-post-date"><time datetime="2026-05-07T04:49:07-05:00">May 7, 2026</time></div>
</div>

<div class="entry-content wp-block-post-content has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-post-content-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="att-video"><div class="att-ytf" data-ytid="n02-puPPMps"><button type="button" class="att-ytf-btn" aria-label="Play video"><img class="att-ytf-thumb" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n02-puPPMps/hqdefault.jpg" srcset="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n02-puPPMps/hqdefault.jpg 480w, https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n02-puPPMps/sddefault.jpg 640w, https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n02-puPPMps/maxresdefault.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 700px) 100vw, 960px" data-fallback="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n02-puPPMps/hqdefault.jpg" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="" width="1280" height="720"><span class="att-ytf-play" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></div></div>



<p class="sqsrte-small wp-block-paragraph">Some of the links in this post are affiliate links, which means I may earn a small commission if you choose to make a purchase at no extra cost to you. As an Amazon Associate, I earn from qualifying purchases. Read my full <a target="_blank" href="/ethics">Ethics &amp; Affiliate Relationship Disclaimer</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you put the M4 Pro MacBook Pro and the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro MacBook Pro</a> side by side, they look basically identical. Same chassis, same keyboard, same speakers, same excellent display, and the same choice of 14-inch or 16-inch sizes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So the real question is not whether Apple redesigned the MacBook Pro. It didn&#8217;t. The question is whether the M5 Pro actually delivers meaningful performance improvements over the M4 Pro, and if those improvements matter in real work.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After comparing the stock configurations of both machines across AI, Xcode, Blender, Logic Pro, video editing, Lightroom, and synthetic benchmarks, the answer is pretty clear: the M5 Pro is better, but mostly in the way a good iterative upgrade is better. It is faster across the board, dramatically better for some AI tasks, and stronger in music production. But if you already own an M4 Pro, this is not the kind of generational leap that makes upgrading feel necessary.</p>



<div class="att-summary"><div class="att-summary-top"><span class="att-summary-title">M4 Pro vs M5 Pro MacBook Pro</span><span class="att-summary-rating"></span></div><p class="att-summary-verdict">The M5 Pro is better, but mostly in the way a good iterative upgrade is better. If you already have an M4 Pro, keep it, but if you are buying new, the M5 Pro looks like the sweet spot in Apple&#8217;s current lineup.</p><div class="att-summary-cols"><div class="att-pros"><div class="att-pc-head">Pros</div><ul><li>M5 Pro SSD reads and writes at about 11,500MB/s vs 5,000MB/s read on the M4 Pro</li><li>Much faster local AI: DiffusionBee took 8.9 seconds vs 18.13</li><li>Logic Pro handled 340 MIDI channels vs 247</li><li>1TB base storage, Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6</li></ul></div><div class="att-cons"><div class="att-pc-head">Cons</div><ul><li>Same design, display, speakers and ports as the M4 Pro</li><li>Starts at $2,200, $200 more than the M4 Pro did</li><li>Gains in Blender, video editing and Lightroom are modest</li><li>Not a big enough leap to upgrade from an M4 Pro</li></ul></div></div><div class="att-summary-buy"><a href="https://geni.us/BpBo4" rel="nofollow sponsored" target="_blank">Check price: M5 Pro MacBook Pro →</a></div></div>



<div class="att-toc"><div class="att-toc-head">In this review</div><ul><li><a href="#The-Hardware-Differences-Between-M4-Pro-and-M5-Pro">Hardware differences</a><ul><li><a href="#cpu-and-gpu">CPU and GPU</a></li><li><a href="#ssd-speed">SSD speed</a></li><li><a href="#storage-and-pricing">Storage and pricing</a></li><li><a href="#memory-bandwidth">Memory bandwidth and RAM</a></li><li><a href="#ports-and-wireless">Ports and wireless</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#Local-AI-Performance-Is-Where-The-M5-Pro-Stands-Out-Most">Local AI</a><ul><li><a href="#diffusionbee">DiffusionBee</a></li><li><a href="#lm-studio">LM Studio with Gemma</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#Xcode-Blender-Show-Solid-But-Not-Dramatic-Gains">Xcode and Blender</a><ul><li><a href="#xcode">Xcode</a></li><li><a href="#blender">Blender</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#Logic-Pro-Strongest-Reasons-To-Consider-M5-Pro">Logic Pro</a><ul><li><a href="#midi-channels">MIDI channel count</a></li><li><a href="#logic-exports">Project exports</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#Video-Editing-Similar-Better-Export-Times-M5-Pro">Video editing</a><ul><li><a href="#magnetic-mask">Magnetic Mask</a></li><li><a href="#final-cut-exports">4K and 30-minute exports</a></li></ul></li><li><a href="#Lightroom-Classic-Performance-Is-Very-Close">Lightroom Classic</a></li><li><a href="#Geekbench-Geekbench AI-and-Cinebench-Results">Benchmarks</a></li><li><a href="#Upgrade-From-M4-Pro-To-M5-Pro?">Should you upgrade from the M4 Pro?</a></li><li><a href="#Why-The-M5-Pro-May-Be-The-Best-Value-In-The-Lineup">The best value in the lineup</a></li><li><a href="#Final-Verdict">Final verdict</a></li></ul></div>



<h2 id="The-Hardware-Differences-Between-M4-Pro-and-M5-Pro" class="wp-block-heading">Hardware differences</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">At a physical level, not much changed. Apple kept the same overall MacBook Pro formula intact:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Same design</li>



<li>Same display quality</li>



<li>Same speaker system</li>



<li>Same keyboard and port layout</li>



<li>Available in both 14-inch and 16-inch models</li>
</ul>



<figure class="wp-block-table att-spec"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>M4 Pro</th><th>M5 Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CPU (stock)</td><td>12-core</td><td>15-core</td></tr><tr><td>GPU</td><td>16-core</td><td>16-core, with neural accelerators</td></tr><tr><td>Memory bandwidth</td><td>273GB/s</td><td>307GB/s</td></tr><tr><td>RAM</td><td>24GB, up to 48GB</td><td>24GB, up to 64GB</td></tr><tr><td>Base storage</td><td>512GB</td><td>1TB</td></tr><tr><td>Internal SSD (my test)</td><td>about 5,000MB/s read, 3,300MB/s write</td><td>about 11,500MB/s read and write</td></tr><tr><td>Ports</td><td>3x Thunderbolt 5, HDMI, SD, MagSafe, headphone jack</td><td>Same</td></tr><tr><td>Wireless</td><td>Previous generation</td><td>N1 chip: Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth 6</td></tr><tr><td>Starting price</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$2,200</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you are deciding between the 14-inch and 16-inch versions, the main difference is still size. The 16-inch also typically has better thermals, which can matter during long sustained workloads. But outside of that, this comparison is really about the chips and internal performance.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/adamtalks.tech/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/macbook-pro-m4-pro-and-m5-pro-laptops-side-by-side-with-model-labels-on-screen-2.webp?ssl=1" alt="The M4 Pro and M5 Pro MacBook Pro side by side"/></figure>



<h3 id="cpu-and-gpu" class="wp-block-heading">CPU and GPU</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The stock M4 Pro configuration tested here has a 12-core CPU. The stock M5 Pro bumps that up to a 15-core CPU. Both machines have 16 GPU cores.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The more interesting change is on the AI side. With the M5 generation, Apple added neural accelerators on every GPU core. The goal is to improve AI performance substantially, with Apple aiming for up to 4x improvement in some workloads.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That full 4x jump did not show up in every AI test, but this part is not just marketing. The M5 Pro clearly performs much better than the M4 Pro in local AI work.</p>



<h3 id="ssd-speed" class="wp-block-heading">SSD speed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This was one of the most dramatic hardware differences in the comparison. On the M4 Pro, SSD performance came in at roughly 5,000MB/s read and 3,300MB/s write. On the M5 Pro, that jumped to about 11,500MB/s read and 11,500MB/s write.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That is a huge leap. For workflows that constantly hit storage, especially loading large media libraries, moving project files, or working with local models and cached assets, that extra SSD speed is a real quality-of-life improvement.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="dr,dw" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h3 id="storage-and-pricing" class="wp-block-heading">Storage and pricing</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Apple also changed the base storage configuration with the M5 lineup. The M4 Pro MacBook Pro started with a 512GB SSD. The <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro MacBook Pro</a> now starts with a 1TB SSD.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That is a welcome upgrade, but it also changes the starting price. The M5 Pro starts at $2,200, while the M4 Pro started at $2,000 with the smaller SSD. So yes, you are paying more. But you are also getting double the storage at the base tier, along with the faster drive.</p>



<h3 id="memory-bandwidth" class="wp-block-heading">Memory bandwidth and RAM</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Apple also increased the memory bandwidth on the M5 Pro, from 273GB/s on the M4 Pro to 307GB/s. That means the unified memory can move data faster between the CPU and GPU, which helps in workloads that rely on both.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Both machines start with 24GB of RAM, but the maximums have changed. The M4 Pro tops out at 48GB, and the M5 Pro goes up to 64GB. If you are buying new and know you need more headroom for larger creative projects or heavier local AI work, that higher memory ceiling does make the M5 Pro more flexible.</p>



<h3 id="ports-and-wireless" class="wp-block-heading">Ports and wireless</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The port selection is unchanged, which is a good thing. Both machines include:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Three Thunderbolt 5 ports</li>



<li>MagSafe charging</li>



<li>Headphone jack</li>



<li>HDMI</li>



<li>SD card slot</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Where Apple did make a meaningful update is wireless connectivity. The M5 Pro includes Apple&#8217;s new N1 chip, which adds Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6. That should translate to faster wireless speeds, better range, and lower energy use for Bluetooth connections.</p>



<h2 id="Local-AI-Performance-Is-Where-The-M5-Pro-Stands-Out-Most" class="wp-block-heading">Local AI</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If there was one area where the M5 Pro consistently separated itself from the M4 Pro, it was AI performance. That lines up with the new neural acceleration built into each GPU core, and the test results backed it up.</p>



<h3 id="diffusionbee" class="wp-block-heading">DiffusionBee</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Using DiffusionBee to generate an image of a cat working on a laptop with nachos on the side, the time dropped from 18.13 seconds on the M4 Pro to 8.9 seconds on the M5 Pro. That is a major reduction in wait time and one of the clearest examples of the M5 Pro&#8217;s AI advantage.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="dif" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h3 id="lm-studio" class="wp-block-heading">LM Studio with Gemma</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In LM Studio running Gemma, there were two key numbers to pay attention to: time to first token and tokens per second. Time to first token improved from 0.97 seconds on the M4 Pro to 0.24 seconds on the M5 Pro. Tokens per second went from 84.5 to 96.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The throughput gain is nice, but the time to first token improvement is the bigger deal in day-to-day use. It makes local AI feel more responsive because the system starts answering much faster.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="ttft,tps" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h2 id="Xcode-Blender-Show-Solid-But-Not-Dramatic-Gains" class="wp-block-heading">Xcode and Blender</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Outside of AI, the M5 Pro generally improved performance by the kind of margins you would expect from a one-generation chip update. Faster, yes. Revolutionary, no.</p>



<h3 id="xcode" class="wp-block-heading">Xcode</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For the Xcode benchmark, compile time dropped from 134 seconds on the M4 Pro to 104 seconds on the M5 Pro. That is a very respectable improvement, especially for developers doing repeated builds every day.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="xc26" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h3 id="blender" class="wp-block-heading">Blender</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Blender gains were more modest. The Classroom scene took 28 seconds on the M4 Pro and 19.47 seconds on the M5 Pro. The more intensive Barbershop scene took 3:41 on the M4 Pro and 3:11 on the M5 Pro.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That is better, but not game-changing. If 3D rendering is the center of your workflow, this is the kind of improvement you appreciate over time rather than instantly feel.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="bcls,bbar" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h2 id="Logic-Pro-Strongest-Reasons-To-Consider-M5-Pro" class="wp-block-heading">Logic Pro</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Music production was one of the most impressive parts of this comparison. In Logic Pro, the M5 Pro handled significantly more load before tapping out.</p>



<h3 id="midi-channels" class="wp-block-heading">MIDI channel count</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The number of simultaneous MIDI channels jumped from 247 on the M4 Pro to 340 on the M5 Pro. That is a substantial increase and a meaningful one for producers running big sessions with lots of instruments.</p>



<h3 id="logic-exports" class="wp-block-heading">Project exports</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On a 64-track project, export time was fairly close: 18 seconds on the M4 Pro and 13 seconds on the M5 Pro. But on a 500-track project, the gap widened more clearly, 2:25 on the M4 Pro vs 1:35 on the M5 Pro.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your sessions are large and plugin-heavy, the M5 Pro offers a much more noticeable advantage than it does in some of the photo and video tasks.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="ltr,l64,l512" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h2 id="Video-Editing-Similar-Better-Export-Times-M5-Pro" class="wp-block-heading">Video editing</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For video editing, the day-to-day experience felt pretty close between the two machines. General editing, multicam work, and timeline responsiveness were solid on both. Neither machine felt like it was struggling. Where the M5 Pro pulled ahead was mostly in exports and certain accelerated effects.</p>



<h3 id="magnetic-mask" class="wp-block-heading">Magnetic Mask</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Applying Magnetic Mask to an 18-second clip took 26 seconds on the M4 Pro and 23 seconds on the M5 Pro. That is an improvement, but not a huge one.</p>



<h3 id="final-cut-exports" class="wp-block-heading">4K and 30-minute exports</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A 3-minute 4K video export took 1:35 on the M4 Pro and 1:20 on the M5 Pro. A 30-minute project export took 15:04 on the M4 Pro and 12:40 on the M5 Pro.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That longer export starts to show the benefit more clearly. So if your workflow regularly involves delivering long-form edits, the M5 Pro does save real time. But for editing feel alone, these two are closer than the spec sheet might suggest.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="fmm,f3,f30" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h2 id="Lightroom-Classic-Performance-Is-Very-Close" class="wp-block-heading">Lightroom Classic</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Photo editing was one of the least dramatic parts of the comparison. In Lightroom Classic, both machines felt about the same for normal interaction such as adjusting exposure and moving through the library.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Importing 500 RAW photos and creating initial previews took 18 seconds on both. Exporting 50 photos to JPEG took 9 seconds on the M4 Pro and 7.23 seconds on the M5 Pro. 200 photos took 34 seconds vs 27.96, and all 500 took 1:15 vs 1:07.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So yes, the M5 Pro is faster. But Lightroom is one of those applications where both machines are already so strong that the difference often feels minor.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="lrp,lr50,lr200,lr500" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="1" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<h2 id="Geekbench-Geekbench AI-and-Cinebench-Results" class="wp-block-heading">Benchmarks</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Synthetic benchmarks matched the broader pattern from the real-world testing. Geekbench 6 single-core was close, 4,000 on the M4 Pro and 4,300 on the M5 Pro, and multi-core went from 21,112 to 26,427. Geekbench AI on the GPU improved significantly on the M5 Pro: the single-precision score went from 16,931 to 19,874, and the half-precision and quantized scores nearly doubled. In Cinebench 2026, the M5 Pro scored 39,317 on the GPU vs 28,796, and 6,794 multi-core vs 5,290.</p>


<div class="att-vs" data-doc="164SUueXKOVqONLQtFEeEvD8n_ixYLyNAM3CANRwPn5k|0" data-macs="M4 Pro MacBook Pro Silver|M5 Pro Macbook Pro Silver 14" data-labels="M4 Pro|M5 Pro" data-charts="gb6m,gb6g,cb26g,aig" data-tests="" data-view="all" data-title="" data-compact="" data-model=""><div class="vs-skel"></div></div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That is exactly what this generation feels like in practice. The M5 Pro is not wildly faster at everything. It is simply better in targeted ways, with AI standing out the most.</p>



<h2 id="Upgrade-From-M4-Pro-To-M5-Pro?" class="wp-block-heading">Should you upgrade from the M4 Pro?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you already own an M4 Pro MacBook Pro, there is really no strong reason to upgrade to the M5 Pro. The M5 Pro is faster, and in some areas it is meaningfully faster. But this is still an iterative update, not a dramatic leap. The biggest benefits show up in:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Local AI tasks</li>



<li>Logic Pro and heavier music production workloads</li>



<li>SSD performance</li>



<li>Some longer exports and compile tasks</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For most people already on M4 Pro, that is not enough to justify replacing a machine that is still extremely capable.</p>



<h2 id="Why-The-M5-Pro-May-Be-The-Best-Value-In-The-Lineup" class="wp-block-heading">The best value in the lineup</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Even though it may not be worth upgrading from an M4 Pro, the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro</a> still comes out looking very strong when compared with the rest of Apple&#8217;s Mac lineup. After testing the M5 Pro alongside machines like the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BoBdo">M5 Max</a>, M4 Max, and standard M4, the M5 Pro felt like the best balance of performance and price.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Max chip is definitely more powerful, but it also costs quite a bit more. And in longer tests, throttling still showed up. In a lot of shorter tests, the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro</a> and <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BoBdo">M5 Max</a> were actually fairly close. That makes the extra cost of the Max harder to justify unless you truly need that additional headroom. I go deeper on that in my <a href="/reviews/m5-pro-vs-m5-max-macbook-pro/">M5 Pro vs M5 Max comparison</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There was also an interesting point in the broader comparison: the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BoBdo">M5 Max MacBook Pro</a> was not doing dramatically better than an upgraded M4 Max Mac Studio in many cases. That makes the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro</a> stand out even more as the smart buy for a lot of professional workflows.</p>



<h2 id="Final-Verdict" class="wp-block-heading">Final verdict</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro MacBook Pro</a> is a very good upgrade over the M4 Pro on paper and in real use. It brings:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>More CPU cores</li>



<li>Much faster SSD performance</li>



<li>Better AI acceleration</li>



<li>Higher memory bandwidth</li>



<li>A higher RAM ceiling</li>



<li>Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">And in testing, it delivered:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Big AI gains</li>



<li>Strong Logic Pro improvements</li>



<li>Better compile and export times</li>



<li>Modest gains in Blender, video editing, and Lightroom</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But the upgrade story depends on where you are starting from. If you already have an M4 Pro, keep it and keep using it. It is still excellent. If you are buying a new MacBook Pro and want the best balance of price and real-world performance, the <a target="_blank" href="https://geni.us/BpBo4">M5 Pro</a> looks like the sweet spot in Apple&#8217;s current lineup.</p>



